WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(audience clapping)

2
00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.339
I am so thrilled to be here.

3
00:00:02.339 --> 00:00:05.120
I want to go to a question

4
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.823
that I have such a burning desire to ask you because

5
00:00:08.823 --> 00:00:11.810
I was reading in your pre-interview and I was like

6
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:16.810
so blown away by your answer and your attitude.

7
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.500
You're a very respected mother.

8
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.500
And you answered what you thought the misconceptions

9
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.330
about being a mom are,

10
00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.710
and I loved your answer.

11
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.290
Will you please enlighten us?

12
00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.570
You know, for me, at the very least

13
00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.380
one of the discoveries that I made upon becoming a mother

14
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.460 line:15%
was that, there's a misconception that

15
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.440 line:15%
if you don't love everything about motherhood

16
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.290
that you don't love your child,

17
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.360
which is not true.

18
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.270
Okay so first of all,

19
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.110
we don't have to act like it's great all the time,

20
00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.706
because sometimes it's really hard.

21
00:00:52.706 --> 00:00:57.090
And as a working mother,

22
00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.010
you know, as a feminist it's important

23
00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.700
for me to support women to make all the choices

24
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.010
that you know that they want to make,

25
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:05.020
even those that I wouldn't make myself.

26
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:09.040
But as a working mother I definitely feel,

27
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.080
there are so many pressures upon us

28
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.670
to feel like we have to do it all,

29
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.825
and some people for example, are born

30
00:01:17.825 --> 00:01:22.580
and having children in their lives gives them a purpose.

31
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.920
They may not have another purpose

32
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.270
that they have found in a way, so it gives them purpose,

33
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.710
and gives them rooting and grounding.

34
00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:29.420
I was not one of those people.

35
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.709
I had other dreams and other purpose that I still maintain

36
00:01:33.709 --> 00:01:35.790
even as a mother.

37
00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.800
So, but what happens is you're still only one person.

38
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:39.633
Yep.
Okay.

39
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:41.390
So you've got all this passion,

40
00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.430
all this purpose still inside you

41
00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:46.400
and yet you also have now all of this responsibility.

42
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.650
Yes.

43
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.100
I haven't heard people put it in the terms

44
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.180
that you're putting it.

45
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.149
I find it so refreshing

46
00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:58.010
because we all are just so hard on ourselves.

47
00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:58.850
The worst.

48
00:01:58.850 --> 00:01:59.770
The worst.

49
00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.270
I would never treat anyone the way I treat myself.

50
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:03.103
Never

51
00:02:03.103 --> 00:02:03.936
So toxic and weird.

52
00:02:03.936 --> 00:02:06.040
I'm like, why do I love everyone?

53
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:10.343
And yet my cup does not runeth over for me.

54
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.580
Very self-critical to a fault, I'm working on it.

55
00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.930
We all are.

56
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.860
But the way you speak it just it's like,

57
00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.190
there are no walls

58
00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.210
and boundaries and barriers in what you're saying,

59
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:28.245
it's just a real truth that I related to as a woman.

60
00:02:28.245 --> 00:02:30.130
Now here's a transition,

61
00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.660
Catherine Zeta Jones is on the show today.

62
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.890
There she is, look at her, wow.

63
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.750
And we had Velma here.

64
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.800
I know that would have been so fun.

65
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.980
If we could have done like a little "Hot Honey Rag" together

66
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.040
I would have loved,

67
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.040
I would have loved to have been her Roxy,

68
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.530
come on, to her Velma.

69
00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.050
Gosh, I would've loved to have seen that.

70
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.950
Now your new album's actually all Broadway songs,

71
00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.253
and obviously Broadway, which I think,

72
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.340
you know, it's funny,

73
00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.400
you would think Broadway might seem insider baseball,

74
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.278
so industry, so theater, so artsy,

75
00:03:04.278 --> 00:03:07.360
I think there's a tremendous amount of the world

76
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.820
and businesses that are taking a nod from Broadway.

77
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.710
I hear a lot of industries saying,

78
00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:16.290
well when Broadway's lights are back on,

79
00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.280
that seems like when we'll open our doors,

80
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.360
because we'll know it's safe for people to congregate

81
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.250
in that way.

82
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.170
Yes, it is absolutely like a vital sign indicator

83
00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.991
of the health of obviously the literal health

84
00:03:29.991 --> 00:03:32.130
within this pandemic of the city

85
00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.190
and and will then be a predicator

86
00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.780
for the health of the economy as we go forward.

87
00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.100
Was this album a bridge to being connected to something

88
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.360
that was cutoff for so many?

89
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.960
In some ways, the timing of this, interestingly enough

90
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.980
I recorded this album with Alex Lacamoire

91
00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.437
who is the arranger and music director and orchestrator

92
00:03:53.437 --> 00:03:56.980
for "Hamilton", "Dear Evan Hansen", "Greatest Showman".

93
00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:57.813
Oh my.

94
00:03:57.813 --> 00:03:58.646
You know, just a few shows,

95
00:03:58.646 --> 00:04:00.510
you might've heard of them, they're all popular.

96
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.353
The best.

97
00:04:02.353 --> 00:04:04.850
And we recorded this album together

98
00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:09.550 line:15%
and we finished recording on March 12th, 2020.

99
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:10.383 line:15%
Oh my gosh.

100
00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:14.030 line:15%
The day that Broadway closed officially.

101
00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.840 line:15%
And so this record has been on the shelf just waiting like,

102
00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.950 line:15%
okay, when will be an opportunity,

103
00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.290 line:15%
like how do we promote anything anymore?

104
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:22.920
I want people to be able to hear it.

105
00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.490
It is not lost on me the symbolism of hopefully

106
00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.600
what this record can be for people,

107
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.450
because we have missed it so much.

108
00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:33.780
We have missed the theater,

109
00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.410
we have missed live entertainment so much,

110
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.280
and there's nothing like live entertainment.

111
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:40.470
There is nothing like it.

112
00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.930
And that's exactly my point,

113
00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:45.283
I hear a lot of people when they talk about concerts,

114
00:04:45.283 --> 00:04:48.970
businesses, true businesses are saying, when Broadway,

115
00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:50.140
we'll follow Broadway.

116
00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.840
And it's coming, it's coming, you know,

117
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.170
like fingers fingers crossed for sure.

118
00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.560
And what I hope is that in the meantime,

119
00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.420
this record can get people excited for what is to come,

120
00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.730
because it definitely, for me has been such a heart project

121
00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.330
in growing up in the theater, always loving this music,

122
00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.774
always wanting an opportunity

123
00:05:06.774 --> 00:05:09.686
to be able to share it with my fans

124
00:05:09.686 --> 00:05:13.090
and to be able to reinterpret some of these classics

125
00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:15.600
in ways that feel like new discoveries for people.

126
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.180
Well in your hands and with your extraordinary partner

127
00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:23.040
I think we're all in for something so wonderful.

128
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.598
It's so fun, 'cause you love theater.

129
00:05:24.598 --> 00:05:25.431
I do.

130
00:05:25.431 --> 00:05:26.750
I really do.

131
00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:27.720
And musicals, who doesn't love musicals?

132
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.190 line:15%
I mean, you're talking "Hamilton" and "Dear Evan Hansen"

133
00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.030 line:15%
and "The Greatest Showman" I've seen all of them.

134
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.280 line:15%
I have all the soundtracks, I play the music,

135
00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:35.293 line:15%
my kids know the lyrics.

136
00:05:35.293 --> 00:05:38.563
So I mean what an amazing team.

137
00:05:38.563 --> 00:05:40.670
Well, thank you, Jennifer,

138
00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:43.330
and may the launch of this album be the beginning

139
00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.790
of a beautiful transition for all of us.

140
00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.570
Yes, I would love that so much.

141
00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.590
And let's, let's go to a show together, how about that?

142
00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:51.423
It's a date.

143
00:05:51.423 --> 00:05:52.790
When Broadway opens up, it's a date.

144
00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:55.130
Okay her new album "Always Like New"

145
00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:56.963
will be available this June.

146
00:05:58.549 --> 00:06:01.216 line:15%
(upbeat music)

